      "text": "To solve for the cost of a pencil, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's define the variables as follows:\n- Let \\( p \\) be the cost of one pen in cents.\n- Let \\( q \\) be the cost of one pencil in cents.\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following two equations:\n1. \\( 4p + 3q = 224 \\) (since \\$2.24 is equivalent to 224 cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5q = 154 \\) (since \\$1.54 is equivalent to 154 cents)\n\nWe will solve this system of equations step by step.\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables. To eliminate \\( p \\), we can multiply the second equation by 2 so that the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations are the same:\n\\[ 2(2p + 5q) = 2(154) \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10q = 308 \\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n1. \\( 4p + 3q = 224 \\)\n2. \\( 4p + 10q = 308 \\)\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[ (4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 308 - 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10q - 4p - 3q = 84 \\]\n\\[ 7q = 84 \\]\n\\[ q = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ q = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is 12 cents. To ensure our solution is correct, we can substitute \\( q = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( p \\). Let's use the first equation:\n\\[ 4p + 3(12) = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 36 = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p = 224 - 36 \\]\n\\[ 4p = 188 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{188}{4} \\]\n\\[ p = 47 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is 47 cents. To verify, we can substitute \\( p = 47 \\) and \\( q = 12 \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2(47) + 5(12) = 94 + 60 = 154 \\]\nThe equation holds true, confirming our solution.\n\nTherefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
