      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that the cost of four pens and three pencils is $\\$2.24$, we can set up the equation for the total cost as follows based on the variables:\n- The cost of one pen is represented by $p$.\n- The cost of one pencil is represented by $q$.\n\nFrom the problem, we have:\n\\[4p + 3q = 224\\] (Equation 1)\n\nAdditionally, given that the cost of two pens and five pencils is $\\$1.54$, we can set up the second equation for the total cost:\n\\[2p + 5q = 154\\] (Equation 2)\n\nWe need to eliminate one of the variables to solve for the other. We can multiply Equation 2 by \\(2\\) to align the coefficients of \\(p\\) with Equation 1:\n\n\\[4p + 10q = 308\\] (Equation 3)\n\nNow, we subtract Equation 1 from Equation 3 to eliminate \\(p\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) &= 308 - 224, \\\\\n10q - 3q &= 84, \\\\\n7q &= 84, \\\\\nq &= \\frac{84}{7}, \\\\\nq &= 12.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pencil is \\(12\\) cents. To find the cost of a\\_ pen, we substitute \\(q = 12\\) back into Equation 1:\n\n\\[\n4p + 3(12) = 224,\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 36 = 224,\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 224 - 36,\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 188,\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{188}{4},\n\\]\n\\[\np = 47.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of one pen is \\(47\\) cents. To confirm our work, let's calculate the total cost of a four pens and three pencils:\n\n\\[\n4 \\times 47 + 3 \\times 12 = 188 + 36 = 224 \\text{ cents}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for the second condition:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 47 + 5 \\times 12 = 94 + 60 = 154 \\text{ cents}.\n\\]\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, and thus the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
